I do not know the instruction for austerities, O best of sages;
if it is fitting for you, instruct me in this secret.
This is what I wish to do; there is no other secret.

bathing should be performed in silence, and worship too in silence;
food should consist of leaves and water at the first two sixth intervals;
and at the third sixth interval, one should be dedicated to fasting.

Thus, at the completion of austerities, the ritual takes place at the sixth interval;
and one should wear the bark of trees and sleep on the bare ground at that time.
Such practice of the vow, performed in silence and called penance, yields fruit.

Praised thus by Brahmā, the maker of the worlds,
Mahāmāyā emerged from the eyes, mouth, nose, arms, and heart of Hari;
assuming a rājasika form, she stood in Brahmā's sight.

And who else is able to bear you, your boar-body, O Lord?
In particular, the earth, impassioned, was violated by you in the water;
subject to the course of women, she conceived a dreadful embryo through your splendor.

that boar-body I wish to abandon, O Śaṃkara;
clinging to enjoyment, it is not possible to abandon it by one's own will.
Now, O Śaṃkara, make me abandon that body by effort.

Then, having tied the ship to that peak, Hari, who bore the form of the Fish,
the Lord of the worlds, went quickly to dry up the waters;
in this manner the Vedas were saved by the wielder of the Śārṅga bow in the form of a fish.

The dissolution out of due time was brought about by the curse of Kapila;
since this unseasonable dissolution was caused by the Lord,
thus all has been told to you in truth, O best of the twice-born.

no obstacle arises in that house, and there will be good offspring;
snakes do not go there, or having gone, they do not bite people;
nor does a promiscuous woman arise in his household.

for him no obstacles arise, nor is he born without sons;
he becomes long-lived, endowed with strength, ever joyous, adept;
he obtains his desired aim and attains the abode of the goddess.

One should worship that supreme god with the mantra of Tatpuruṣa.
In this way, the seat of the great-souled Jalpīśa confers merit;
knowing this thus, a man goes to the supreme state of Śaṅkara.

To the south of Mount Candrakūṭa is Mount Janārdana;
to the south, in its lower part, is the pool named Aśvakrāntā.
There is no sacred bathing place equal to it within the sphere of the cosmic egg;
those who die here in water or on land go to the eternal Brahman.
On Mount Janārdana, Viṣṇu, assuming his own form as the Tortoise,
remains there having split the stone, served by gods and gandharvas.
